small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124304 #edb/ref 1074 "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/da/daf4111656a525abf51bf58105831ed11378ed7fc52915e1e58ca5f75884bcf0.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/be/bed6471ce9aed8eb16a0d35f1d9985b8e99941f7c1326645de971960bdb5aedd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124305 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124305 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](images/95/957c59d27a871a52f110c3dca91cec390091c13ceba6e14d6485f92aa150c51a.svg)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](images/60/6095f1a65a9b9b245de2dd4cad611d2616a2b3b15e4701fe33c3c53b913c2283.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93b466b2-9b8d-55fa-87b8-b5bf24a497cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124305 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](images/41/41bd0d4956d8ec09d0339a79329c6739e07e698d9dee9f192968d77c9ec28842.svg)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](images/30/30a1bdc2316f5d7acc527cb1d69fc11c7b732f9e22a4d57a11ed84f2db1e3cd5.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/56/5606e00ccc39829e3c599aa86286dcc1f1cb4e3febb280adf4e1bbe18dc7ab6a.svg)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124306 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124306 #edb/ref 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](images/46/4676de86bd5b4bc2a8e1d6a9432379d0769163113913137f8143a71eb16e8240.svg)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5b5534-2082-593e-af07-df83497ea84c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124306 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124307 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124307 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ![](images/07/076634439afec4c0e4fb0a443d88163245269988e323ddb9d83d1a4a1b18139e.svg) and ![](images/b2/b2fd30034f45fb2d8b06f2697f137c93de21942822b08193c3e640ef51d4e7c0.svg) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124307 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0929855b-0310-5c87-8cff-cc2faa8a5ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124307 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/dd/dd733ebebc7131eb2762e6e98e1d14d07a730e5e37657c639ae9cc46be123ee2.svg)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124491 #edb/ref 